## Summary

The CSS parser looks for the first `!important` anywhere in a declaration value. When it appears inside a string or `url()`, the value is cut at that point and the declaration is marked important, which prints broken CSS:

```css
/* input */
.a { content: "a !important b"; }
.b { background: url("x!important.png") !important; }

/* output */
.a { content: "a !important; }
.b { background: url("x !important; }
```

Custom properties hit the same path (`--foo: "!important"` becomes `--foo: " !important`).

Per [css-syntax-3](https://www.w3.org/TR/css-syntax-3/#consume-declaration) the important flag is only the last `!` followed by `important` in the declaration, so this checks for `!important` at the end of the trimmed value instead.

## Test plan

Added a css-parser test with `!important` inside a string, inside `url()` and inside a custom property value. It fails on `main` and passes with this change. `vitest run` in `packages/tailwindcss` passes (5164 tests).

I used Claude Code to write this change.

## Summary

When a theme uses a prefix (e.g. `@import "tailwindcss" prefix(tw)`), the pre-`color-mix(…)` fallback for opacity modifiers loses its opacity. For example, `tw:text-red-500/50` generates:

```css
.tw\:text-red-500\/50 {
  color: var(--tw-color-red-500); /* opacity is gone */
}

@supports (color: color-mix(in lab, red, red)) {
  .tw\:text-red-500\/50 {
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--tw-color-red-500) 50%, transparent);
  }
}
```

Browsers that support `color-mix(…)` are unaffected, but browsers without support (e.g. Safari < 16.2) render the color at full opacity. The polyfill is enabled by `@tailwindcss/postcss`, so prefixed projects using it hit this.

The polyfill resolves the variable in the theme to inline its value. The variable name in the AST includes the prefix (`--tw-color-red-500`), but theme values are stored unprefixed (`--color-red-500`), so the lookup always missed and the whole `color-mix(…)` was replaced by its first color argument.

This PR unprefixes the variable name before the lookup, which makes `Theme#unprefixKey` public. The output now matches the non-prefixed case:

```css
.tw\:text-red-500\/50 {
  color: #fb2c3680;
}
```

## Test plan

1. Added a test next to the existing `color-mix(…)` polyfill tests in `packages/tailwindcss/src/index.test.ts` using `@theme prefix(tw)` and `tw:text-red-500/50`.
2. Verified the test fails without the fix (fallback is `var(--tw-color-red-500)`) and passes with it (fallback is `#fb2c3680`, same as the non-prefixed test).
3. `pnpm vitest run` → 82 files, 5657 tests passed.
4. `pnpm exec prettier --check` on the changed files passes.

## Summary

`@tailwindcss/vite` only hands a stylesheet to Tailwind when it uses `@apply`, `@plugin`/`@config`, `theme(…)`, `@tailwind utilities` or `@variant`. A stylesheet that only uses `@theme` is returned as-is:

```css
/* src/theme.css, linked from index.html */
@theme {
  --color-brand: red;
}

.brand {
  color: var(--color-brand);
}
```

`vite build` emits this file unchanged, so the browser gets the raw `@theme` block and `--color-brand` is never defined. Compiling the same CSS with `tailwindcss` directly (or with `@tailwindcss/postcss`) gives `:root, :host { --color-brand: red; }` and the `.brand` rule.

`Features.AtTheme` was added in #18979 for this case, but the Vite plugin's check wasn't updated. This adds `Features.AtTheme` to it.

## Test plan

- Added `production build with a stylesheet that only uses @theme` to `integrations/vite/index.test.ts`. On `main` it fails because the CSS still contains the `@theme { … }` block; with this change the output has the `:root, :host` declaration and the `.brand` rule.
- `pnpm vitest --root=./integrations run vite/index.test.ts -t 'only uses|production build|demote'`: 4 passed.
- `prettier --check` on the changed files.

## Summary

Unused `@keyframes` from `@theme` are removed from the output. Usage is only tracked from `animation` declarations, so keyframes that are referenced through `animation-name` are removed even though the stylesheet uses them:

```css
@import 'tailwindcss';

@theme {
  @keyframes spin {
    to {
      transform: rotate(360deg);
    }
  }
}

.spinner {
  animation-name: spin;
  animation-duration: 1s;
}
```

The output keeps `.spinner` but has no `@keyframes spin`, so the animation never runs. Writing the same thing as `animation: spin 1s` keeps the keyframes.

This treats `animation-name` declarations the same way as `animation` when collecting the used keyframe names.

## Test plan

- Added `keyframes are generated when used in an animation-name` to `packages/tailwindcss/src/index.test.ts`. On `main` the snapshot is missing `@keyframes used`; with this change it passes, and `@keyframes unused` is still removed.
- `pnpm vitest run` in `packages/tailwindcss`: everything passes except tests that hit their timeout while the machine was under load (`variants with the same root are sorted deterministically` times out on `main` too).
- `prettier --check` on the changed files.

## Summary

`@vitejs/plugin-vue` gives `<style module>` blocks ids that end in `&lang.module.css` (e.g. `App.vue?vue&type=style&index=0&lang.module.css`). `isPotentialCssRootFile` only accepts `&lang.css`, so these blocks are never handed to Tailwind and `@reference` / `@apply` end up in the built CSS unprocessed:

```vue
<template>
  <div :class="$style.foo">Hello Vue!</div>
</template>

<style module>
  @reference './main.css';

  .foo {
    @apply text-red-500;
  }
</style>
```

With Vite 8.3.4, `@vitejs/plugin-vue` 6.0.10 and `@tailwindcss/vite` 4.3.3 the bundle contains:

```css
@reference './main.css';
._foo_rfnam_4 {
@apply text-red-500;
}
```

Plain `<style>` and `<style scoped>` blocks in the same component are processed.

This matches `&lang.module.css` in the transform filters and in `isPotentialCssRootFile`. The Tailwind transform runs with `enforce: 'pre'`, so `@apply` is expanded before `vite:css` hashes the class names.

## Test plan

- Added `production build with <style module> blocks` to `integrations/vite/vue.test.ts`. On `main` it fails because the CSS still contains `@apply text-red-500;`; with this change it passes and the hashed `.foo` rule has `color: var(--color-red-500, …)`.
- `pnpm vitest --root=./integrations run vite/vue.test.ts`: 5 passed.
- `pnpm vitest --root=./integrations run vite/`: all Vue and CSS modules tests pass. On my machine, dev-server tests that are unrelated to this change (`external source file changes update the CSS`, react-router `build mode`) timed out under load.
- `prettier --check` on the changed files.
